WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.630
- Hi everyone Sal Khan here
from from Khan Academy .

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.990
Welcome to our daily homeroom which is

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:42.780
our way of staying in touch.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.390
It started with with obviously

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.790
all the school closures and
social distancing with COVID.

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:48.470
But now it's really just evolved

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.500
into an interesting forum
to have good conversations

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.910
about education and other things.

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.690
Before we get into our conversation

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:57.270
with today's guests that I'm very excited

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:58.490
to be speaking to.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:01.960
I will start with my
standard announcement,

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.540
or what I always say, which is a reminder

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.580
that Khan Academy is a not-for-profit

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.790
that we can only exist with
philanthropic donations

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:10.990
from folks like yourself.

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:12.500
So if you're in a position to donate

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.330
please think about doing so.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:15.830
I wanna give a special shout out

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:17.580
to several corporations that stepped-up

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.560
especially once they saw that we were

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.530
already running at a deficit pre-COVID.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:22.740
And that our deficit had grown

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.420
in order to support the fact that

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.080
our traffic was 250, 300% normal,

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:27.913
and we're trying to accelerate

00:02:27.913 --> 00:02:31.270
a whole bunch of programs
to keep everyone learning.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:32.700
A special thanks to Bank of America,

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.560
Google.org, AT&amp;T, fastly, and Novartis.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.880
So, thanks to all of them,
but I do remind everyone,

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:41.210
we're still running at a deficit.

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:42.310
So if you're in a position to do so

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:44.220
please think about donating.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.160
So with that, I'm really excited

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:49.520
to invite our guest Rachel Skiffer.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.890
Rachel, there's many
ways to introduce you.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:57.360
You are the Head of School
for my children's school.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.030
That is one way.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:04.030
You run Khan Lab School, which
I helped get off the ground,

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.730
but you really are doing
the real heavy lifting

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:08.380
you and the team there.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:11.120
And also, I think you have
just a broad background

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.650
as an educator, you know you were

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:18.430
the Head of Strategy at Philips Andover,

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:20.250
for many years you've
worked in admissions,

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:21.520
you've worked as a lawyer, you've worked

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:22.670
as a strategy consultant.

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:24.030
So very broad background.

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:26.610
So Rachel, thank you,
thank you for joining us.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.110
- Thank you for having me Sal.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.280
- So a couple of, there's a bunch of stuff

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.440
you know when we originally scheduled

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.600
you know one of the things I thought

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:37.680
would be really interesting to talk

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:40.520
about is how (murmurs).

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:41.810
Well maybe a good place to start

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:45.640
just to ground us is explain to me

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:47.590
and to those watching you know,

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:50.920
why do you think a school
like Khan Lab School

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:53.020
needed to exist or what was interesting

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.060
about it to you that you know you wanted

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:56.310
to become the Head of School?

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.350
And then I'd love to go into how you've

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:01.520
been able to navigate the
COVID school closure situation?

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:02.760
And then also talk about a lot

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:06.460
of the the environment that
we're not finding ourselves in.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:07.293
- Sure.

00:04:07.293 --> 00:04:10.190
So there are lots of things that drew me

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:11.200
to Khan Lab School.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.473
I would say, one of the things

00:04:13.473 --> 00:04:15.360
that was the most important to me

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.030
was this underlying assumption

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:21.990
that all students could
achieve at a very high level.

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:24.750
And that because we give students

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:28.470
the space and time to
really master material,

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:33.250
without the outside pressure
of arbitrary letter grades,

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:36.100
allowing them a lot of autonomy

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.970
and the ability to work
with other students

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:40.420
and other teachers.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:45.400
The motivation for our
students is really internal

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.970
it's not pressure from parents,

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:52.800
but really the kids are motivated

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.990
because they want to learn.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:57.550
And I also think the year round model

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:00.160
for us is really exciting.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:03.010
So even when parents are
interested in the school

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:04.260
and they ask a little bit about

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:06.350
our summer term and what that means,

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:09.380
and what the kids be sad,
that they don't have summer.

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:11.400
What I explained to them is,

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.980
school shouldn't be so bad for nine months

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:17.020
that students need summer as an escape.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:18.910
But if school is really exciting

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:21.470
for the whole year
students are really excited

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:23.070
to come even during the summer

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:26.010
and it helps with things
like knowledge loss

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.520
and other things that happen when schools

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:30.943
are just closed for three months.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:34.190
- And obviously I'm biased here,

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:36.990
but I can say as a
parent that it's strange

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:38.380
when it's a full day school.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:40.790
So the school day ends at five or 6pm.

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:42.880
The kids don't wanna leave.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:44.890
- They don't wanna leave.

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:46.850
- They don't like breaks (laughs).

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:49.050
- No they don't really
like it when they're sick,

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.290
we kinda have to say it's time to go,

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:53.570
but they try to pretend they're okay.

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:55.490
It's the opposite of probably what

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:56.920
some other schools have to deal with

00:05:56.920 --> 00:06:00.393
when kids are malingering,
but our kids they love it.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:05.410
- And what you know, what has been

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:06.790
I guess there's a couple like pre-COVID.

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:09.200
What has been, you know you kinda takeaway

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.190
have some of those beliefs
have been confirmed?

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:13.290
What's been difficult, but I think

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.890
given the world that we're
in a lot of educators

00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:17.890
or a lot of places have struggled

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:20.600
with virtualization or
whatever school closures,

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:21.900
and then we're going into another

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.050
back to school season, which is also going

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:25.133
to be very uncertain.

00:06:27.046 --> 00:06:31.880
What's your analysis of how KLS has fared

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:33.400
through this school closures?

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:34.680
What do you think y'all have done

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:38.100
that have worked out well,
and what are other learnings?

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.480
- So I think some of the things

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:41.790
that have worked out well, they've worked

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:43.750
out well because their processes

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:47.110
and practices that we've always had.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:51.150
And so for instance, things
like student autonomy

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:55.380
for our youngest students,
say second graders,

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:57.960
third graders, they'll meet with a teacher

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.330
who's an advisor and they'll meet with

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:01.800
that person, once a week for 30 minutes

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.960
to map out their week.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:07.050
What they're gonna study,
when they're gonna study,

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:09.730
when they're gonna spend
time on passionate projects.

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:13.640
But we really focus on
things like executive skills,

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:15.930
in addition to social emotional learning,

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:18.550
but really those skills
that are so important

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:21.360
when people leave school,
but that most schools

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:25.250
don't talk about or help
support students in developing.

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:27.820
So even though we are traditionally

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:30.250
a brick and mortar school, and right

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:32.480
we're not an online school naturally

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:34.040
just some of the technology that

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:37.340
our students use, and are also responsible

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:41.650
for in terms of handing in
work, tracking their progress.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:45.340
When school moved from
the building to home.

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:49.450
A lot of our students,
even the younger ones again

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:53.410
had exercised that muscle
of planning out their work,

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:57.253
handing things in using
an electronic forum.

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:01.520
And so I think that's
been really beneficial

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:02.973
for our students.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:05.770
And I think also for our older students

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:07.860
particularly our seniors, we have our

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:10.493
first graduating class coming up.

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:13.470
We didn't have to have a whole debate

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:15.270
about how are we gonna grade the students,

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:16.570
if they're not here.

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:19.410
We use a mastery based system, which means

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:23.070
that students can move forward

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:24.790
once they've mastered material.

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:29.340
And so what we say it's
effectively sort of a 90%,

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:30.920
of mastery of all the material

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:32.800
so that they don't have gaps.

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:35.490
So students are able to
focus on the learning

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:39.420
and not oh, is this B plus,
can I get it to an A minus

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:42.610
or you know, I'm just
not gonna show up anyway,

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:43.990
because I'm not doing well.

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:47.430
We allow students the space
to really achieve and do well.

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:49.573
- Right, right.

00:08:49.573 --> 00:08:51.540
And I have to say, you
know what's interesting

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.190
for anyone if you visit the school,

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:55.210
what's unusual or what
people find it unusual

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:57.780
is you'll even see seven
and eight year olds,

00:08:57.780 --> 00:08:59.590
who have a very clear
idea of what they need

00:08:59.590 --> 00:09:00.740
to do at any given time.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:02.500
And you know even though
it might be associated

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:05.350
with me or Khan Academy, a lot of ways

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:07.660
it's not a technology focused school,

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:10.430
it's about student
agency and collaboration

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:12.440
and human interaction.

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:13.610
So you see these kids being able

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:17.530
to take that agency and I know there's

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:20.950
a question from YouTube
Adnan Abbas Kanchwala

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:23.740
about, you know why do you need a KLS,

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:25.570
if you have things like Khan Academy

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:27.500
that kids could use whenever they want?

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:29.150
And I'll actually take a stab at that

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:33.550
the logic behind founding Khan Academy was

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:34.920
sorry founding Khan Lab School

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:36.520
is that Khan Academy exists, and hopefully

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:39.180
you can scale and empower
hundreds of millions

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:40.560
or billions of folks.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.630
But the existence of
tools like Khan Academy

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:45.790
allows us to re-imagine what the

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:48.820
actual school day or school
structure could look like.

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:50.650
And so some of the things
that Rachel talked about,

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:52.890
you know why do we have to
take off during summers?

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.450
Why can we go all year round?

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:56.460
Can't we get to a competency based model?

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:57.970
Can't we create a world where

00:09:57.970 --> 00:09:59.943
the students teach each other,

00:09:59.943 --> 00:10:03.470
where faculty are able to co-teach,

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:06.590
where they're able to do
one-on-one smaller group sessions.

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:08.720
And so the whole point of
it and why it's a Lab School

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:09.960
is so that it can explore these

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.307
and eventually share with the world.

00:10:12.307 --> 00:10:13.660
And I have to say, Rachel you know

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:16.670
you and the team at Khan
Lab School as a parent,

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:18.870
it has been amazing how
seamless it has been.

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:19.900
But, and I think to a large degree,

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:21.690
because it is y'all have been reinforcing

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:25.850
these ideas of student autonomy agency,

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:28.160
and these really personal connections

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:29.013
between the teachers and the students,

00:10:29.013 --> 00:10:31.700
which I think is super powerful.

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:34.210
One question, you know
there's a bunch of questions

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:38.060
people are asking about
this coming school year.

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:38.893
- Right.

00:10:38.893 --> 00:10:40.190
- You know, what do you think
is gonna happen broadly,

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:42.200
to just someone who observes
the education space?

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:44.380
I know, you talk to a
lot of heads of schools

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:46.620
around the country and principals,

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:48.713
and how are y'all thinking about it?

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:54.370
- So we're doing a lot of
scenario planning, right?

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:57.890
And so we are the sort of the scenario

00:10:57.890 --> 00:10:59.660
where everything goes back to normal

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:01.360
and we're all in the building

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:03.650
as we were before, which
is really put on the shelf,

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:06.210
'cause that's not likely to happen.

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:08.590
And so now we're thinking about

00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:12.060
where our students are
developmentally where a K to 12.

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:15.170
So what do our new five year olds,

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:17.550
our kindergarteners,
what are they gonna need?

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:20.990
And what kind of support
as students move up

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:23.220
through that chain through senior year?

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:25.840
How much time if we're able to

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:27.260
can they spend on campus?

00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:31.430
And then how do we do some
of that teaching remotely?

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:34.520
And I like to go back
to the earlier question

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:37.690
about sort of, why do you
need a Khan Lab School,

00:11:37.690 --> 00:11:41.090
and it's just because
learning is relational, right?

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:44.880
And when as students develop, they move

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:49.330
from this mode to building
a foundation, right?

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:51.870
Learning how to learn habits of mind,

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:54.100
but content is important.

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:55.880
And even though there are a lot of things

00:11:55.880 --> 00:11:58.890
that are at a student's fingertips.

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:03.260
It's much easier to grow
in your math development,

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:05.850
if there are things like
times tables that you know,

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:07.700
and you can recall instead of having

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:10.800
to look those up every
time you have a question.

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:14.410
And so the same way, if I just put a piano

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:18.700
in a kid's house, you
know it's actually helpful

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:21.570
for them to have someone
with a deep expertise,

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:24.250
who knows kids well, to really teach them

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:25.810
and build scaffolding.

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:27.510
And so the students practice and

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:31.780
then they're eventually able
to learn pieces on their own.

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:33.750
So I think what we've discovered,

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:37.610
even though everyone is in their own home

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:39.870
or apartment learning is that it's

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.320
the community piece that's really helped.

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:45.300
And the fact that we know
our students really well,

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:47.850
we've had enough time to check in not only

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:50.260
with our teachers, but with our families,

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:53.770
to see who had the necessary technology

00:12:53.770 --> 00:12:57.210
and internet connections
at home and who didn't,

00:12:57.210 --> 00:12:59.360
so that we could make sure that everyone

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:03.000
had what they needed by the time

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:04.660
sort of school went remote.

00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:08.860
So for the fall, we're doing a mix of

00:13:08.860 --> 00:13:10.860
sort of health and wellness and

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:13.840
what the County of Santa
Clara is telling us

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:17.630
as possible in terms
of physical distancing,

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:19.420
masks, things like that.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:21.373
And then we're also thinking about,

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:24.640
where our students are in terms of

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:27.540
what they're able to do on their own.

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:28.870
And then we're also thinking about

00:13:28.870 --> 00:13:32.060
the social aspects because even you know,

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:34.520
the most brilliant student who probably

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:37.540
doesn't need teachers anymore.

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:38.580
Even though they're really helpful

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:40.940
to help us teach other students

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:42.770
you still need that social aspect.

00:13:42.770 --> 00:13:45.590
There's still so much about growing up

00:13:45.590 --> 00:13:48.790
that is about learning how
to assist with other people

00:13:48.790 --> 00:13:53.790
and negotiate and the
moments in between classes

00:13:53.830 --> 00:13:55.637
there's as much value in there I think

00:13:55.637 --> 00:13:58.270
for students as what
they're learning in class.

00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:01.280
So to get to your questions Sal.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:03.580
I think we're looking at
sort of a hybrid model

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:07.700
of having students at
home, having students

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:10.530
come to campus when possible, and thinking

00:14:10.530 --> 00:14:15.530
about how to make that
as seamless as possible.

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:18.933
So we'll see.

00:14:18.933 --> 00:14:21.170
- And one thing that
actually, we should find time

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:22.570
to brainstorm on and more,
but I'm excited about it

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:25.410
'cause even when we started
KLS, I was hoping that

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:27.210
a lot of the school day
could happen outside

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:30.023
but now we have a
epidemiological reason for that.

00:14:33.596 --> 00:14:35.895
(murmurs) just a tree and a hotspot.

00:14:35.895 --> 00:14:38.199
(both laugh)

00:14:38.199 --> 00:14:40.390
And you know it's, people don't realize

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:43.099
the word Academy actually
comes from a field

00:14:43.099 --> 00:14:46.920
where Plato used to teach

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:49.560
or have Socratic dialogues outside,

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:51.960
and then it became his Akadēmos.

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:54.610
I'm a big fan especially
if the weather's good.

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:55.860
Even if the weather's bad frankly,

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:57.389
I think it builds character.

00:14:57.389 --> 00:14:58.613
- You know, the kids are tough.

00:14:58.613 --> 00:15:00.530
They can handle it.

00:15:00.530 --> 00:15:01.780
It's California.

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:02.613
We'll be fine.

00:15:02.613 --> 00:15:03.446
- That's right.

00:15:03.446 --> 00:15:04.279
And even if it's not California,

00:15:04.279 --> 00:15:06.291
it's you know, we're
meant to be in nature.

00:15:06.291 --> 00:15:07.124
- Right.

00:15:07.124 --> 00:15:07.957
- So and there were a
ton of questions here

00:15:07.957 --> 00:15:08.910
about the education model.

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:10.443
But I do wanna actually
broaden our conversation

00:15:10.443 --> 00:15:13.230
because I think there's another obviously,

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:15.410
what's been happening
with Black Lives Matter,

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:17.260
with the George Floyd murder.

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:20.900
And what I found your
letter to the community,

00:15:20.900 --> 00:15:24.420
I thought was, it was reflective,

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:27.180
and it gave justice to
the moment we're in.

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:29.650
But it was also I found
almost strangely hopeful

00:15:29.650 --> 00:15:31.950
and had a silver lining to it.

00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:34.220
So I guess one question that I think

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:36.820
a lot of folks are struggling with

00:15:36.820 --> 00:15:39.000
especially parents of young kids are,

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:42.220
how do we talk about what's
going on in the world?

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:45.660
Well, how do we talk
about police brutality,

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:48.283
racism in this moment?

00:15:49.564 --> 00:15:51.323
- You know, that's an
interesting question.

00:15:51.323 --> 00:15:55.430
I have preschoolers and
so the the conversation

00:15:55.430 --> 00:15:58.400
I have not as an educator, but as a parent

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:02.473
with my husband is sort of
what do they already know?

00:16:03.970 --> 00:16:06.200
And how can we best support them?

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:10.400
So I'd say for us as an
African American family

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:12.690
one of the things that we make sure of

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:15.610
is that in terms of the books
that we have in the house,

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:18.800
that my kids see themselves
represented, right?

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:20.620
And it's not necessarily books

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:22.800
about the civil rights movement,

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:25.540
or you know people who broke barriers,

00:16:25.540 --> 00:16:29.210
but also about kids in
Central Park hanging out

00:16:29.210 --> 00:16:30.810
with a Dinosaur, right?

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:34.600
Or kids who bring a Lion to the library.

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:38.980
But there's something about, and educators

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:42.400
talk a lot about mirrors and windows.

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:44.700
And so there's a part of the population

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:46.760
where they see themselves mirrored

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:48.320
in everything, right?

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:51.543
TV shows, games, the books that they have.

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:55.530
And then there's some kids
that only have windows.

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:59.630
They're very good at
understanding cultures

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:01.390
that are different than theirs

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:03.510
particularly if they're
not in the majority,

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:05.850
but they don't see a lot of mirrors.

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:09.710
And so for us in terms
of sort of building up

00:17:10.870 --> 00:17:13.090
our kids self worth, it's really

00:17:13.090 --> 00:17:15.980
about making sure they
have as many mirrors

00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:20.160
as possible in their day to
day lives, including schools.

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:24.020
So you know what books
are in the school library

00:17:24.020 --> 00:17:27.080
and what does the student
population look like.

00:17:27.080 --> 00:17:28.603
For older students.

00:17:30.290 --> 00:17:32.393
If I can put my educator hat back on,

00:17:32.393 --> 00:17:36.370
there've been so many
book lists going around

00:17:36.370 --> 00:17:40.000
for parents to read, older students

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.267
to read along with them.

00:17:42.267 --> 00:17:45.017
"Stamped" is a great book
that I would recommend.

00:17:45.017 --> 00:17:47.480
"The New Jim Crow" has
a young readers edition

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:50.333
and also a teacher's guide.

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:54.810
But I also think, one it's important

00:17:56.520 --> 00:17:58.970
whether you're a parent or a teacher,

00:17:58.970 --> 00:18:01.580
when you're having conversations

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:05.110
the point isn't to
necessarily re-traumatize

00:18:05.110 --> 00:18:08.470
particularly your Black
and Brown students.

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:12.240
So if you're a history teacher,

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:14.150
and you're talking about Emmett Till

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:16.900
you don't necessarily have to show

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:19.270
the picture of him in the coffin, right?

00:18:19.270 --> 00:18:22.110
Our humanity is not in our death.

00:18:22.110 --> 00:18:23.550
But you can have a picture of him

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:27.503
when he was alive, a vibrant young man.

00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:32.280
And the same thing with
the books that are read.

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:34.790
I think there's a lot
about American history

00:18:34.790 --> 00:18:38.010
that people in the U.S. don't know.

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:40.500
So, for parents, what do you know

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:43.470
about reconstruction at the time when

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:45.440
African-Americans were governors

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:48.150
and senators (murmurs) towns

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:51.150
in addition to understanding things

00:18:51.150 --> 00:18:54.340
like the Tulsa race massacre.

00:18:54.340 --> 00:18:58.853
So it would be I think disrespectful,

00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:04.870
for me to at this point lack hope,

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:06.780
just in thinking about allowing the work

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:09.110
of the people who came before me

00:19:09.110 --> 00:19:12.053
even in my family, my
grandparents, my parents,

00:19:13.540 --> 00:19:16.730
and so for now I'm hopeful but I know

00:19:16.730 --> 00:19:18.130
that people have to do work.

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:22.510
Another good book "The
Condemnation of Blackness"

00:19:22.510 --> 00:19:24.380
I think everyone has to check sort

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:28.540
of their underlying
presumptions about people.

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:31.330
And the one of the things that brings me

00:19:31.330 --> 00:19:33.320
the most hope is just looking at

00:19:33.320 --> 00:19:37.550
the people who are
demonstrating and protesting.

00:19:37.550 --> 00:19:40.980
It's some of the most multicultural

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:46.940
and I think there are a lot
of coalition's being built.

00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:51.500
So just the people who are
in the street protesting

00:19:52.555 --> 00:19:56.820
police brutality really
focused on Anti-racism.

00:19:56.820 --> 00:19:58.860
It's probably the most diverse group

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:01.950
of people that this country has ever seen.

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:04.043
And it's happening around the world.

00:20:05.470 --> 00:20:06.303
- Yeah.

00:20:06.303 --> 00:20:07.210
And what's your advice?

00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:09.260
You know, this is something
I'm struggling at home

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:13.750
especially the younger
kids, you know all my kids

00:20:13.750 --> 00:20:16.050
but you know Azad and Diya who are five

00:20:16.050 --> 00:20:17.690
and almost nine years old.

00:20:17.690 --> 00:20:21.290
You know they know something's going on

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:23.260
and they're curious they
might even have seen

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:27.030
some of the footage of you know,

00:20:27.030 --> 00:20:28.910
I hope they haven't seen the footage

00:20:28.910 --> 00:20:31.670
of the George Floyd murder

00:20:31.670 --> 00:20:34.590
but you know how do
you, 'cause there's one

00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:36.533
of this the graphic nature of some of it,

00:20:36.533 --> 00:20:38.530
(murmurs)

00:20:38.530 --> 00:20:40.970
at some age, maybe they should see it.

00:20:40.970 --> 00:20:42.610
But then there's also a question of

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:43.820
you know for some of
them, they're just like,

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:45.180
wait this makes no sense at all.

00:20:45.180 --> 00:20:47.540
Like why should anyone be you know,

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:49.950
based on their skin, it just doesn't grok

00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:51.670
with their head and
you're almost introducing

00:20:51.670 --> 00:20:53.973
the idea of racism to them.

00:20:55.050 --> 00:20:56.940
- I mean, I think the reality is

00:20:56.940 --> 00:21:01.360
that even though, they're young kids

00:21:01.360 --> 00:21:05.370
and young kids have a very well
developed sense of justice.

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:10.150
They know it's not fair we still live,

00:21:10.150 --> 00:21:11.630
in this country, right?

00:21:11.630 --> 00:21:14.690
So there are things that
are just there in the walls.

00:21:14.690 --> 00:21:17.573
They're systemic, that we don't question.

00:21:18.850 --> 00:21:22.810
But that still color how
we interact with people.

00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:27.097
So when one of the conversations or

00:21:27.097 --> 00:21:28.650
if you wanna talk to your kid about it

00:21:28.650 --> 00:21:30.910
is just to ask them what they know,

00:21:30.910 --> 00:21:32.350
what have they heard?

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:34.890
And I think people would be surprised

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:38.640
by how much their children have picked up.

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:41.920
If they haven't actively
had these conversations

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:43.030
in the house.

00:21:43.030 --> 00:21:46.720
Just you know kids are
smart, and they get by

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:49.370
with a lot of things because
they know how to listen.

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:55.440
And so I think that you can't assume

00:21:55.610 --> 00:21:59.017
that kids don't see color, right?

00:21:59.017 --> 00:22:00.520
One is 'cause they don't.

00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:01.720
But two there are a lot of studies

00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:03.960
that say even when kids
are babies they do.

00:22:03.960 --> 00:22:06.530
So then the question is, is the difference

00:22:06.530 --> 00:22:09.110
a bad thing or a good thing?

00:22:09.110 --> 00:22:11.740
And so those are the kind of conversations

00:22:11.740 --> 00:22:12.853
that you can have.

00:22:14.470 --> 00:22:19.430
And I think there's also the conversation

00:22:19.430 --> 00:22:22.098
about even sort of the
most innocuous things

00:22:22.098 --> 00:22:26.090
that people assume about
different groups of people.

00:22:26.090 --> 00:22:30.993
So can you have that
conversation and be brave?

00:22:30.993 --> 00:22:33.210
And have you done enough work and study

00:22:33.210 --> 00:22:36.040
as a parent to be able to answer

00:22:36.040 --> 00:22:38.120
some of those questions and even questions

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:40.070
some of those assumptions yourself.

00:22:40.070 --> 00:22:43.310
It's not easy, right?

00:22:43.310 --> 00:22:46.410
I did some professional development

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:48.640
once with someone who said that

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:50.540
sort of Anti-racism work, it's like going

00:22:50.540 --> 00:22:51.670
to the dentist, right?

00:22:51.670 --> 00:22:53.043
It's not one and done.

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:56.170
You gotta flush, you
gotta brush in between

00:22:56.170 --> 00:22:57.520
and every once in a while you gotta go in

00:22:57.520 --> 00:22:58.550
and get a deep cleaning.

00:22:58.550 --> 00:23:03.530
So it's something you
have to be conscious about

00:23:03.530 --> 00:23:04.480
and deliberate about.

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:06.430
Because if you're not, then you just slip

00:23:06.430 --> 00:23:10.113
into to bad habits and old habits.

00:23:11.260 --> 00:23:13.250
- Yeah, that's super helpful.

00:23:13.250 --> 00:23:14.310
But it's obviously yes.

00:23:14.310 --> 00:23:16.880
It's not easy and it's very nuanced.

00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:17.713
There are some questions.

00:23:17.713 --> 00:23:19.290
And these conversations go so fast

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:21.700
when we're talking about
interesting topics.

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:23.630
There are some definitely some questions.

00:23:23.630 --> 00:23:28.630
Let's see, Marshmello
from YouTube is asking,

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:31.040
and maybe we can, you know broaden this,

00:23:31.040 --> 00:23:33.540
how will COVID evolutionize learning?

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:36.450
I don't think we will be
very normal very soon.

00:23:36.450 --> 00:23:37.340
And what's the new normal?

00:23:37.340 --> 00:23:40.090
So I guess maybe to
paraphrase this question,

00:23:40.090 --> 00:23:41.790
there's a stuff that you're
doing the immediate term to,

00:23:41.790 --> 00:23:44.150
you know make sure that
everything is working,

00:23:44.150 --> 00:23:45.710
so to speak and back to school,

00:23:45.710 --> 00:23:48.810
but how do you think this is
going to change education?

00:23:48.810 --> 00:23:51.180
Well one at KLS maybe
in a more permanent way

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:52.013
even post-COVID.

00:23:52.013 --> 00:23:53.280
Or what implications do you think

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:56.210
it might have broadly,
you know well beyond KLS

00:23:56.210 --> 00:23:57.600
in the system as a whole.

00:23:57.600 --> 00:23:58.870
And maybe I'll even add, you know

00:23:58.870 --> 00:24:00.870
what's going on with the
protests Black Lives Matter,

00:24:00.870 --> 00:24:01.850
do you think that's also going

00:24:01.850 --> 00:24:03.380
to have some impact on curricular

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:04.800
or things like that, but COVID,

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:07.403
both of these things that
are going on in the world?

00:24:08.391 --> 00:24:11.750
- So I would say in terms
of what is COVID doing,

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:16.100
I think it's shining a
light on how unprepared

00:24:16.100 --> 00:24:18.263
we are for change.

00:24:19.660 --> 00:24:21.880
And I also think it shines a light

00:24:21.880 --> 00:24:26.063
on how incredibly creative
teachers can be in a moment.

00:24:27.180 --> 00:24:30.223
But that calls for a lot of support.

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:35.180
And not just putting a laptop or an iPad

00:24:35.180 --> 00:24:37.350
and a teacher's hand or a student's hand,

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:40.770
but what kind of real training

00:24:40.770 --> 00:24:44.280
that's best led by teachers and teachers

00:24:44.280 --> 00:24:47.770
who are really excited
about this kind of teaching,

00:24:47.770 --> 00:24:51.160
and that they can work
with their peer group

00:24:51.160 --> 00:24:52.303
and their colleagues.

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:58.160
I also think all of this shines a light

00:24:58.160 --> 00:25:01.300
on the fact that the teaching actually

00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:03.883
should follow the student where they are.

00:25:05.910 --> 00:25:10.910
And so giving less weight to just how long

00:25:12.190 --> 00:25:14.800
has a student sat in a class,

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:17.950
but more what have they
actually learned in that class?

00:25:17.950 --> 00:25:22.480
And is it okay for them to
take a little bit longer

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:24.900
to make sure they master the material,

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:28.190
then to make sure that just you know

00:25:28.190 --> 00:25:30.010
things are running on
time and if you don't

00:25:30.010 --> 00:25:32.860
get it too bad kid, we have
to move to the next thing.

00:25:32.860 --> 00:25:34.800
Like is that the point?

00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:37.510
So, at Khan Lab School you know

00:25:37.510 --> 00:25:42.510
we always say that we're here
to educate and not to sort.

00:25:44.250 --> 00:25:47.530
And again, I think the underlying belief

00:25:47.530 --> 00:25:49.790
that all students can achieve at a

00:25:49.790 --> 00:25:52.700
really high level is somewhat radical.

00:25:52.700 --> 00:25:54.770
There's good research to say that

00:25:54.770 --> 00:25:59.140
when you're able to combine
mastery based learning,

00:25:59.140 --> 00:26:01.810
where the goal for kids
is to actually understand

00:26:01.810 --> 00:26:04.090
the material versus getting a grade.

00:26:04.090 --> 00:26:07.240
And you're able to do

00:26:08.880 --> 00:26:13.880
some kind of one-on-one time
not tutoring necessarily

00:26:14.030 --> 00:26:16.170
but even sort of tools like Khan Academy

00:26:16.170 --> 00:26:20.840
are incredible for kids
to shore up their skills,

00:26:20.840 --> 00:26:24.420
that just the achievement is incredible.

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:26.950
So I think I'm really having belief

00:26:26.950 --> 00:26:30.830
in all kids and allowing
more space and time.

00:26:30.830 --> 00:26:34.510
And I'd say also, there
are probably millions

00:26:34.510 --> 00:26:36.850
of parents across this nation who have

00:26:36.850 --> 00:26:41.450
a deeper appreciation for
teachers and what they do,

00:26:41.450 --> 00:26:43.163
because it's hard.

00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:46.260
Whether you're teaching
someone how to read

00:26:46.260 --> 00:26:50.070
or whether you're teaching someone

00:26:50.070 --> 00:26:51.870
about socks or something like that.

00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:56.240
It's all really difficult and it's about

00:26:56.240 --> 00:26:57.810
making sure that the motivation

00:26:57.810 --> 00:27:00.443
for students is internal.

00:27:01.990 --> 00:27:05.743
Because we do a lot of hoop
jumping, I think these days.

00:27:06.700 --> 00:27:08.270
- Yeah completely agree.

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:09.730
And you know, my own take is exactly

00:27:09.730 --> 00:27:10.563
what you're saying is I think

00:27:10.563 --> 00:27:13.870
there's a world where
post-COVID the whole system

00:27:13.870 --> 00:27:16.770
you know, this hybrid environment we're in

00:27:16.770 --> 00:27:19.940
it is actually, it's
making a lot of educators,

00:27:19.940 --> 00:27:21.830
a lot of systems have to
think a little bit more

00:27:21.830 --> 00:27:23.630
in the direction that y'all have already

00:27:23.630 --> 00:27:26.010
been thinking at KLS which
is, how do we give students

00:27:26.010 --> 00:27:27.500
more agency but at the same time

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:30.240
give them constraints and accountability,

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:33.120
so that they are able to add supports,

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:35.800
with teachers but you know, rethinking.

00:27:35.800 --> 00:27:37.300
Okay, is lecture the best thing

00:27:37.300 --> 00:27:39.040
or is it better for that teacher time

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:41.570
to be more in small group
focused interventions

00:27:41.570 --> 00:27:43.640
or one on one sessions and you know

00:27:43.640 --> 00:27:45.400
goal reflections and things like that,

00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:49.900
and I have to say, you
know I've been bragging

00:27:49.900 --> 00:27:51.560
maybe more than I should because obviously

00:27:51.560 --> 00:27:53.090
my name is associated with the school,

00:27:53.090 --> 00:27:54.660
so it probably doesn't reflect well on me.

00:27:54.660 --> 00:27:57.840
But y'all have done an incredible job.

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:00.570
Being able to fluidly go between these,

00:28:00.570 --> 00:28:03.020
you know in person
world, and this you know

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:05.160
socially distance world, I think can be

00:28:05.160 --> 00:28:07.130
a really good example for what

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:10.500
could be done other places.

00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:11.990
Rachel, unfortunately, we're out of time.

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:14.490
I hope we can do this
again in the near future,

00:28:14.490 --> 00:28:15.870
'cause there's a ton of questions

00:28:15.870 --> 00:28:18.310
that I didn't even get to.

00:28:18.310 --> 00:28:23.120
I will just say, you know
I get a an undue amount

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:26.840
of credit for both Khan Academy
and for Khan lab school,

00:28:26.840 --> 00:28:30.759
because of, I guess
there's a name association.

00:28:30.759 --> 00:28:31.854
- I do.

00:28:31.854 --> 00:28:33.451
(Khan laughs)

00:28:33.451 --> 00:28:36.680
- But Rachel is one of
the people in my life,

00:28:36.680 --> 00:28:39.370
who's really you know, doing the real

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:42.380
on the groundwork of
driving this broader vision

00:28:42.380 --> 00:28:44.860
and this broader mission forward.

00:28:44.860 --> 00:28:47.110
And so I feel very lucky to work with her,

00:28:47.110 --> 00:28:48.780
not just as the Head of School

00:28:48.780 --> 00:28:50.220
of my children's school, which I do feel

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:52.900
very fortunate about, but also you know

00:28:52.900 --> 00:28:55.670
really pushing forward these ideas

00:28:55.670 --> 00:28:58.390
of what school could be not
just for the 200 students

00:28:58.390 --> 00:29:01.110
at KLS but what they
could be for the you know

00:29:01.110 --> 00:29:03.810
a billion and a half kids
around the world one day.

00:29:03.810 --> 00:29:06.520
So thank you Rachel for that.

00:29:06.520 --> 00:29:07.353
- Sure.

00:29:07.353 --> 00:29:08.186
Thank you for having me.

00:29:08.186 --> 00:29:11.370
It's a pleasure to to be
your child's Head of School

00:29:11.370 --> 00:29:13.020
your children's Head of School.

00:29:13.020 --> 00:29:14.660
Next time we can talk about synchronous

00:29:14.660 --> 00:29:16.050
versus asynchronous learning.

00:29:16.050 --> 00:29:18.200
I'm sure you got some of those questions.

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:20.790
But I think it's a great
time for education.

00:29:20.790 --> 00:29:23.750
And I think, as a country, we're poised

00:29:23.750 --> 00:29:26.915
to have some fundamental change

00:29:26.915 --> 00:29:28.315
for the better of all of us.

00:29:29.210 --> 00:29:32.170
- Actually not to, you know we
had a nice conclusion going,

00:29:32.170 --> 00:29:34.870
but since you brought up
asynchronous and synchronous,

00:29:35.710 --> 00:29:37.140
'cause there are several
questions about that.

00:29:37.140 --> 00:29:37.973
How do you structure?

00:29:37.973 --> 00:29:39.210
I mean, how are you thinking about

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:41.560
asynchronous and synchronous
just to (murmurs).

00:29:42.558 --> 00:29:43.391
- We're doing both.

00:29:43.391 --> 00:29:46.360
I think initially, when everyone had

00:29:46.360 --> 00:29:48.440
to shelter in place the instinct was

00:29:48.440 --> 00:29:50.530
to sort of take what you
were doing in the building,

00:29:50.530 --> 00:29:54.610
and then just map it
to an online platform.

00:29:54.610 --> 00:29:56.493
And it just doesn't work.

00:29:58.220 --> 00:29:59.640
Even for the teachers, the students

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:01.290
you have to to move
your body a little bit.

00:30:01.290 --> 00:30:04.570
So then the conversation is, what is great

00:30:04.570 --> 00:30:06.900
for students to be able to do together?

00:30:06.900 --> 00:30:09.540
What are the conversations
that they can have?

00:30:09.540 --> 00:30:10.910
And then what are those moments

00:30:10.910 --> 00:30:14.160
where they can actually
do some work on their own?

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:15.810
And it's actually better for them

00:30:15.810 --> 00:30:19.410
to have some space for
flow and for deep thinking.

00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:22.290
So for us we are doing both

00:30:23.420 --> 00:30:28.010
from kindergarten all the
way through 12th grade.

00:30:28.010 --> 00:30:33.010
And our teachers are incredibly
thoughtful and creative.

00:30:34.568 --> 00:30:37.520
And even when I look at sessions
for the kindergarteners,

00:30:37.520 --> 00:30:40.210
they're actually engaged.

00:30:40.210 --> 00:30:41.610
But I think part of it is that

00:30:41.610 --> 00:30:45.500
in those moments they're being
asked to interact, right?

00:30:45.500 --> 00:30:48.010
It's not just our
teachers talking to them,

00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:49.313
but they're asking them questions.

00:30:49.313 --> 00:30:52.033
They're asking the
students to work together.

00:30:53.060 --> 00:30:54.940
And so that happens in math classes,

00:30:54.940 --> 00:30:57.400
english classes, history
classes, science classes.

00:30:57.400 --> 00:31:01.030
So I think you need both and I think just

00:31:01.030 --> 00:31:06.030
by virtue of it being online,
it can't be as full a day,

00:31:06.260 --> 00:31:08.540
as we're used to students having

00:31:08.540 --> 00:31:10.790
when they're in person,
just because they need

00:31:10.790 --> 00:31:12.450
those breaks.

00:31:12.450 --> 00:31:14.550
But it doesn't mean they're learning less.

00:31:15.918 --> 00:31:18.390
If the system that you
have in place works,

00:31:18.390 --> 00:31:20.900
and if anything it's teaching them again

00:31:20.900 --> 00:31:24.483
those executive skills,
time management planning,

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:28.433
that are really gonna
serve them beyond school.

00:31:30.267 --> 00:31:31.470
- Now and this is one of the areas

00:31:31.470 --> 00:31:34.340
you know one of the dreams of KLS

00:31:34.340 --> 00:31:37.760
is that it can really work in
symbiosis with Khan Academy.

00:31:37.760 --> 00:31:38.830
And, you know one of the things

00:31:38.830 --> 00:31:40.320
that I'm excited about
exploring with Rachel

00:31:40.320 --> 00:31:41.890
and the team at KLS over the
next couple of months are,

00:31:41.890 --> 00:31:43.760
you know a lot of these practices

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:45.770
that Rachel and KLS team have developed

00:31:45.770 --> 00:31:47.490
can we help document some of that?

00:31:47.490 --> 00:31:49.100
Because the whole world is trying

00:31:49.100 --> 00:31:50.390
to answer these questions right now.

00:31:50.390 --> 00:31:52.840
And so thanks Rachel and the team

00:31:52.840 --> 00:31:56.300
for really I think doing a
really amazing job right now.

00:31:56.300 --> 00:31:59.557
And, you know thanks for
being guests right now.

00:31:59.557 --> 00:32:02.119
- Sure thanks for the support Sal.

00:32:02.119 --> 00:32:03.557
Thanks.

00:32:03.557 --> 00:32:04.693
- Thanks Rachel.

00:32:06.800 --> 00:32:08.490
So thanks, everyone for joining us.

00:32:08.490 --> 00:32:10.650
As you can see, I'm usually lucky

00:32:10.650 --> 00:32:13.680
in getting people to
show up for this thing

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:15.140
and having interesting conversations

00:32:15.140 --> 00:32:18.160
and the time always seems
to go a little bit faster

00:32:18.160 --> 00:32:20.190
than I expect and my apologies

00:32:20.190 --> 00:32:22.240
to all of the questions
that I did not get to,

00:32:22.240 --> 00:32:25.240
but I'm sure we'll be able to
get Rachel on in the future.

00:32:25.240 --> 00:32:27.800
But for everyone watching
thanks for watching,

00:32:27.800 --> 00:32:29.450
and I remind you if you're in a position

00:32:29.450 --> 00:32:32.130
to do so please think about
donating to Khan Academy

00:32:32.130 --> 00:32:33.563
but I will see you tomorrow.

